INT. MILL OWNER'S PARLOR, LANCASHIRE - NIGHT

1842. A rich, close room with a coal fire. Heavy curtains, a horsehair sofa, a mahogany sideboard. The clatter of the mill beyond the wall never stops.

MR. EZEKIEL HARGREAVES-OLUWASEUN (50s, black frock coat, gold watch chain) stands at the fire with his back to the room. His wife, ABIGAIL HARGREAVES (40s, plum silk, a sewing basket in her lap), sits on the sofa working a needle. Before them, standing very straight with his cap crushed in both hands, is a young man from the mill floor: a YOUNG WEAVER (20s, thin, clogs, flax-colored hair).

HARGREAVES
Your name?

YOUNG WEAVER
Tom, sir.

HARGREAVES
Tom what?

YOUNG WEAVER
Tom Aldous, sir.

HARGREAVES
And you are here to complain.

TOM
I'm here to ask, sir.

HARGREAVES
Ask what?

TOM
That the looms be stopped for an hour at noon.

HARGREAVES
Why?

TOM
So we can eat, sir.

Abigail's needle pauses. She doesn't look up.

HARGREAVES
You eat at the bench.

TOM
We can't, sir. The dust.

HARGREAVES
Then you eat in the yard.

TOM
There's no yard, sir. There's the canal.

HARGREAVES
Then you eat on the canal.

TOM
(a beat)
That's not a joke, sir?

HARGREAVES
Mr. Aldous, I do not joke.

ABIGAIL
(quietly)
Ezekiel.

HARGREAVES
Abigail, this is not the moment.

ABIGAIL
It is exactly the moment.

She sets down her needle. Stands. The firelight catches the gold at her throat.

ABIGAIL (CONT'D)
Mr. Aldous, how many hours do you work?

TOM
Fourteen, ma'am.

ABIGAIL
And how many weavers are there?

TOM
Two hundred and forty, ma'am.

ABIGAIL
And they all want the same thing?

TOM
They do.

HARGREAVES
(together)
Abigail, sit down.

ABIGAIL ^
(together)
Ezekiel, the man is asking for an hour.

Their voices cross. Hargreaves breaks first, turns to the fire, and stares into it.

HARGREAVES
An hour a day at two hundred and forty hands is a significant loss.

ABIGAIL
Is it more than you spent on that clock?

HARGREAVES
Abigail.

ABIGAIL
The one in the hall. Eighty guineas.

HARGREAVES
It keeps accurate time.

ABIGAIL
So does the mill whistle.

Silence. Tom stares at the carpet. The mill clatters through the wall. The fire pops.

HARGREAVES
(at last)
Mr. Aldous.

TOM
Sir.

HARGREAVES
Return to your loom. Tell the others there will be no stoppage.

TOM
Sir--

HARGREAVES
But tomorrow, at noon, bring out your bread and we shall see what the dust does to it.

TOM
(stunned)
Sir?

HARGREAVES
Out, Mr. Aldous.

Tom bows awkwardly and backs out. The door closes behind him. Abigail lowers herself onto the sofa, picks up the needle, and begins to stitch again with great care. Hargreaves turns from the fire and looks at her for a long moment.

HARGREAVES (CONT'D)
That clock was a mistake.

ABIGAIL
It was a very good clock.

HARGREAVES
It was.
